// ============================================================
// DiscipleTrack
// MVP Database ERD — Version 2
// ============================================================
//
// Source of truth for the proposed DiscipleTrack PostgreSQL schema.
//
// This is a database design artifact, not the final Supabase migration.
//
// ERD v2 incorporates findings from:
// - Identity / Church / D Group review
// - Gathering / Attendance review
// - Discipleship Progress review
// - Monitoring / Follow-up review
//
// Revised by the Discipleship Meeting Attendance revision
// (ADR-009): meeting participant attendance outcomes, the
// RECORDED participation state, role-specific monitoring sources
// and CONSECUTIVE_MISSED_MEETINGS (the last two superseded by
// ADR-014, see the 2026-10-05 revision). Applied migrations are not
// rewritten; the revision reaches the database through a new
// migration in the Discipleship Meeting + Progress slice.
//
// Revised by Vertical Slice 3 (Ministry Structure, Migration
// 006): d_group_invitations and its status enum, the per-church
// group name rule, and the D Group membership and assignment
// constraints noted on each table.
//
// Revised 2026-10-02 (ADR-010, ADR-011): lesson content is
// delivered by the app (its table is added by the Curriculum /
// Lesson Content slice after the material is inspected); lesson
// readiness is an explicit submission, recorded on
// disciple_lesson_progress as ready_at and submitted_by;
// required_meetings no longer drives progression and its meaning
// is an open product decision.
//
// Revised 2026-10-05 (ADR-012, ADR-014):
// - D Group gatherings and gathering attendance are removed from
//   the MVP (ADR-014). d_group_gatherings, gathering_attendance and
//   gathering_status still exist in applied Migrations 001 and 002
//   and stay defined here, marked deprecated with no MVP owner.
// - One monitoring condition, CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE, now meaning
//   consecutive explicitly recorded ABSENT discipleship meeting
//   outcomes for a Disciple (ADR-014 decisions 6, 7).
//   CONSECUTIVE_MISSED_MEETINGS, planned by the ADR-009 revision
//   above, was never applied and is withdrawn; the "role-specific
//   monitoring sources" in that revision are superseded.
// - church_settings: consecutive_absence_threshold redefined;
//   consecutive_missed_meeting_threshold dormant (ADR-014
//   decisions 12, 13).
// - DISCIPLE and DISCIPLER may coexist; Discipler eligibility after
//   confirmed COMPLETED of Lesson 5; ministry_role_transitions is
//   the appointment record; self-pairing prohibited (ADR-012,
//   enforced since Migration 013).
// - "Missed meeting" wording and percentage examples removed.
//
// Revised 2026-10-05 (ADR-015): the Discipler marks a lesson
// completed in one step, with no Leader confirmation. No column or
// type changes: complete_lesson() sets COMPLETED, completed_at,
// confirmed_by, and reuses ready_at (= completed_at) and
// submitted_by (= confirmed_by). READY_FOR_COMPLETION is dormant.
//
// Revised 2026-10-05 (user decision, ninth): the curriculum has ten
// lessons, not twelve. No column or type changes; the Slice 5
// migration replaces the bootstrap functions for the count (see
// curricula and curriculum_lessons).
//
// As built, Vertical Slice 5 (Journey / Meeting Progress), no table,
// column or type added beyond what is noted on each table:
// - Migration 007 (20261002000001): schema revisions (participation
//   status RECORDED, attendance_status, submitted_by, the Progress
//   State Check), RLS, recording, completion and undo, reads.
// - Migration 008 (20261006000001): meeting and participant voids;
//   get_meeting_history() returns per-row void permissions.
// - Migration 009 (20261006000002): reopen_lesson_completion(), a
//   database-only recovery operation (ADR-016).
// - Migration 010 (20261006000003): list_group_progress() for the
//   group's Leader and the Coordinator.
// - Migration 011 (20261006000004): no meeting minimum (ADR-017);
//   the placeholder lesson_meeting_policy() and the count-based void
//   protection are dropped.
// Revised 2026-10-06 (ADR-016): a completion is locked once
// legitimate progress exists in a later lesson.
// Revised 2026-10-06 (ADR-017): N1 closed, no minimum number of
// meetings; meeting count never determines completion.
//
// As built, Vertical Slice 6 (D Group Assignment and Discipler
// Progression, ADR-018):
// - Migration 012 (20261006000005): d_group_placements (direct
//   placement, one active group per person, "Needs setup");
//   d_group_invitations retired and kept as history.
// - Migration 013 (20261006000006): discipler_basis enum and
//   d_group_memberships.discipler_basis;
//   church_settings.initial_setup_closed_at; DISCIPLE now excludes
//   LEADER only; no self-pairing, no reciprocal pairing.
// - Migration 014 (20261006000007): Discipler eligibility derivation
//   and appoint_discipler(); ministry_role_transitions read policies.
// - Migration 015 (20261006000008): an appointment locks undo of the
//   eligibility lesson and earlier lessons.
//
// ============================================================


// ============================================================
// IDENTITY / FOREIGN KEY CONVENTION
// ============================================================
//
// DiscipleTrack intentionally uses three identity levels.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. profiles.id
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Represents the authenticated application user.
//
// Used for actor fields:
//
// created_by
// recorded_by
// assigned_by
// placed_by
// ended_by
// approved_by
// confirmed_by
// performed_by
// resolved_by
// voided_by
// actor_user_id
//
// These answer:
//
// "Which authenticated user performed this action?"
//
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. church_memberships.id
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Represents a person's long-lived identity inside a church.
//
// Used when domain information belongs to the person within
// that church.
//
// Examples:
//
// discipleship progress
// meeting participation and recorded attendance outcomes
// attention conditions
// follow-ups
//
// These records survive:
//
// - D Group transfers
// - Discipler reassignment
// - D Group leadership changes
//
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. d_group_memberships.id
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Represents historical D Group membership and responsibility.
//
// Used when a relationship specifically depends on the person's
// contextual responsibility inside a D Group.
//
// Examples:
//
// - Discipler → Disciple assignment
// - responsible Discipler on a discipleship meeting
//
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// GENERAL NAMING RULE
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// *_by / *_user_id
//     → profiles.id
//
// church_membership_id
// *_church_membership_id
//     → church_memberships.id
//
// *_d_group_membership_id
//     → d_group_memberships.id
//
// These identity levels must not be interchanged simply because
// they ultimately represent the same human being.
//
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ACTOR vs RESPONSIBILITY
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A third class of field records ongoing responsibility rather
// than a completed action.
//
// Example:
//
// follow_ups.assignee_church_membership_id
//
// This answers:
//
// "Who is currently responsible for this care?"
//
// It does NOT answer:
//
// "Who performed this action?"
//
// Responsibility fields use church_memberships.id because the
// responsibility belongs to a person within a church, must remain
// inside that church, and must stay visible when that person's
// membership becomes INACTIVE, TRANSFERRED or ARCHIVED.
//
// Actor fields (*_by) continue to use profiles.id.
//
// ============================================================


// ============================================================
// ENUMS
// ============================================================

Enum church_status {
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}


Enum membership_status {
  PENDING
  ACTIVE
  INACTIVE
  TRANSFERRED
  ARCHIVED
}


Enum church_role {
  ADMIN
  COORDINATOR
}


Enum d_group_status {
  ACTIVE
  INACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}


Enum d_group_responsibility {
  LEADER
  DISCIPLER
  DISCIPLE
}


// Why a DISCIPLER row exists (Migration 013, ADR-018 decision 6).
Enum discipler_basis {
  INITIAL_ROLLOUT [note: 'recognized as an Existing Discipler at setup while the initial setup window was open']
  LEADER_SELF [note: 'the group Leader added themselves']
  APPOINTMENT [note: 'appointed by the Coordinator after the eligibility lesson; see ministry_role_transitions']
}


// Historical (Migration 012, ADR-018): only d_group_invitations uses
// it, and no operation writes that table any more.
Enum d_group_invitation_status {
  PENDING
  ACCEPTED
  DECLINED
  WITHDRAWN
  EXPIRED
}


// Deprecated (ADR-014): used only by d_group_gatherings, which has no
// MVP owner. Retained because Migration 001 created it; dropped only
// after a separate explicit decision, after the gathering tables.
Enum gathering_status {
  DRAFT
  FINALIZED
  CANCELLED
}


// Used by discipleship_meeting_participants (Record Meeting).
// gathering_attendance also references it but is deprecated (ADR-014).
// All four values are kept; their meaning for meetings is in
// DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 11 (ADR-009 outcome table, ADR-011).
Enum attendance_status {
  PRESENT
  ABSENT
  LATE
  EXCUSED
}


Enum curriculum_status {
  ACTIVE
  INACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}


Enum discipleship_meeting_status {
  RECORDED
  VOIDED
}


// Record validity only, mirroring discipleship_meeting_status.
// Whether a participation earns progress credit is derived from this
// state together with attendance_status; see
// discipleship_meeting_participants.
//
// RECORDED was named COUNTED in ERD v2 and in Migration 001. The rename
// happens in a new migration; Migration 001 is not rewritten.
Enum meeting_participation_status {
  RECORDED
  VOIDED
}


Enum lesson_progress_status {
  NOT_STARTED
  IN_PROGRESS
  READY_FOR_COMPLETION [note: 'dormant: no operation enters it (ADR-015); kept because Migration 001 is immutable']
  COMPLETED
}


Enum attention_condition_status {
  ACTIVE
  RESOLVED
}


// One MVP condition, one source, one threshold (ADR-014):
//
// CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE
// → consecutive explicitly recorded ABSENT outcomes on RECORDED
//   participant rows of RECORDED discipleship meetings, for a
//   DISCIPLE subject, within the current discipler assignment
//   episode, ordered by (occurred_at, meeting id), across lesson
//   boundaries. ABSENT increments; PRESENT and LATE break; EXCUSED
//   breaks and is never an absence (BR-027a). NO RECORD is never
//   ABSENT. User-facing label: "consecutive recorded absences".
//
// CONSECUTIVE_MISSED_MEETINGS is withdrawn (ADR-014 decision 7). It
// was a planned value, never applied by any migration, and is
// removed here because this DBML describes the intended schema.
// CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE is not renamed.
Enum attention_condition_type {
  CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE
}


Enum follow_up_status {
  REQUIRED
  IN_PROGRESS
  RESOLVED
}


// Same meaning as attention_condition_type.CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE
// (ADR-014). The planned CONSECUTIVE_MISSED_MEETINGS value is
// withdrawn (ADR-014 decision 7); it was never applied.
Enum follow_up_reason {
  CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE
}


Enum follow_up_action_type {
  CONTACTED
  SENT_MESSAGE
  CALLED
  PERSONAL_CONVERSATION
  SCHEDULED_VISIT
  OTHER
}


Enum follow_up_resolution_type {
  CARE_COMPLETED
  CONDITION_CORRECTED
  ADMINISTRATIVE_CORRECTION
}


Enum announcement_scope {
  CHURCH
  D_GROUP
}


// ============================================================
// 1. IDENTITY
// ============================================================
//
// Supabase manages auth.users.
//
// profiles.id conceptually references auth.users.id.
//
// The Supabase auth table is not fully recreated in this ERD.
// ============================================================

Table profiles {
  id uuid [pk, note: 'Same UUID as Supabase auth.users.id']

  full_name varchar [not null]

  phone varchar
  avatar_url varchar

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Application-level profile for an authenticated user.

  Authentication credentials remain managed by Supabase Auth.

  full_name is required. Registration collects it and supplies it
  through Supabase Auth user metadata, which a trusted trigger uses
  when creating this row. A missing or blank value is rejected rather
  than replaced with a placeholder.

  This table intentionally does NOT store:

  - church system role
  - D Group responsibility
  - D Group assignment
  - consecutive recorded absences
  - current lesson
  - lesson completion counts

  Those belong to their respective domains.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 2. CHURCH
// ============================================================

Table churches {
  id uuid [pk]

  name varchar [not null]

  join_code varchar [not null, unique]
  join_code_updated_at timestamptz

  status church_status [not null, default: 'ACTIVE']

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents a church workspace.

  The MVP initially launches for one local church.

  Church ownership remains explicit so the database does not
  permanently assume that only one church can ever exist.

  A join code allows users to request membership in the church.

  A join code must never grant privileged roles automatically.

  join_code format (DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 1):

  ^[A-HJ-NP-Z2-9]{10}$

  Ten characters from a 32-symbol alphabet that excludes I, O, 0 and 1.
  Enforced by CHECK churches_join_code_format_check. Generated
  cryptographically by private.generate_join_code(); bootstrap and
  future regeneration validate any supplied code against the same
  pattern.

  join_code is never readable by a client. Column-level SELECT for
  authenticated covers id, name and status only; anon has no grant.
  Lookup happens inside lookup_church_by_join_code(), which returns
  only id and name.

  join_code_updated_at records when the current code was set, at
  bootstrap or by a later regeneration.

  Row visibility: a person sees their own church only, while their
  membership there is PENDING or ACTIVE (RBAC section 1a). A PENDING
  member reads the name of the church they requested and nothing
  else about it.

  status ACTIVE → ARCHIVED is the only transition. ARCHIVED is terminal
  in the MVP and means the workspace is closed; it is not a normal
  single-church operation.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 3. CHURCH MEMBERSHIP
// ============================================================

Table church_memberships {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_id uuid [not null]
  user_id uuid [not null]

  status membership_status [not null, default: 'PENDING']

  joined_at timestamptz

  approved_by uuid
  approved_at timestamptz

  onboarding_completed_at timestamptz

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    (church_id, user_id) [unique]
  }

  Note: '''
  Represents a user's long-lived membership in a church.

  Field semantics (DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 1):

  created_at
  → when the join request was made

  joined_at
  → the first time the membership became ACTIVE; never overwritten
    by a later reactivation or reinstatement

  approved_by / approved_at
  → the approver and the moment of approval, written only by
    approve_church_membership(); NULL on rejection and on the
    bootstrap membership, which is provisioned rather than approved

  onboarding_completed_at
  → when the member completed the one-time first-entry welcome, set
    once by complete_onboarding(); NULL until then. Server-backed so a
    reinstall or another device never replays the welcome. Not reset
    by reactivation.

  Writes happen only through controlled operations:
  request_join_church(), approve_church_membership(),
  reject_church_membership(), complete_onboarding() and the future
  membership-management operations. Clients have no INSERT or UPDATE
  path.

  This becomes the stable ministry identity used by domains such as:

  - discipleship progress
  - meeting participation and recorded attendance outcomes
  - monitoring
  - follow-ups

  D Group membership is intentionally separate.

  A person's church identity therefore survives D Group transfers.

  One person has exactly one membership row per church, enforced by
  UNIQUE(church_id, user_id). A returning member reactivates the
  existing row rather than receiving a second one, so their
  meeting history, progress and care history remain continuous.

  Lifecycle:

  PENDING     → ACTIVE (approval) | ARCHIVED (rejection)
  ACTIVE      → INACTIVE | TRANSFERRED | ARCHIVED
  INACTIVE    → ACTIVE
  TRANSFERRED → ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED    → ACTIVE (reinstatement, controlled and audited)

  TRANSFERRED means the person transferred out of this CHURCH.

  Moving a Disciple between D Groups is a different concept and must
  not alter church_memberships.status.

  Reactivation does not restore previous D Group responsibilities.
  Those live in d_group_memberships and are already ended.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 4. SYSTEM / CHURCH ROLES
// ============================================================

Table church_role_assignments {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  role church_role [not null]

  assigned_by uuid [not null]

  started_at timestamptz [not null]
  ended_at timestamptz

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Stores privileged church-level roles.

  MVP roles:

  ADMIN
  COORDINATOR

  Normal church membership does not require a MEMBER role row.

  Church/system roles are intentionally separate from D Group
  responsibilities.

  Example:

  Sarah may be:

  COORDINATOR at church level

  AND

  LEADER of Young Adults A.

  Active-role uniqueness will be implemented through PostgreSQL
  constraints / partial indexes where appropriate.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 5. D GROUPS
// ============================================================

Table d_groups {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_id uuid [not null]

  name varchar [not null]
  description text

  status d_group_status [not null, default: 'ACTIVE']

  created_by uuid [not null]

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  archived_at timestamptz

  indexes {
    (church_id, `lower(trim(name))`) [unique, note: 'WHERE status <> ARCHIVED']
  }

  Note: '''
  Represents one D Group.

  name is required and not blank, and unique within the church
  ignoring case and surrounding spaces among groups that are not
  ARCHIVED. An archived group releases its name.

  Every group has exactly one active LEADER from creation:
  create_d_group() creates the group and the LEADER row together,
  and assign_d_group_leader() replaces the Leader in one
  transaction. See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 2.

  D Groups do not require a fixed recurring meeting schedule.
  DiscipleTrack does not schedule group events; D Group gatherings
  are not part of the MVP (ADR-014).

  Lifecycle, Coordinator-controlled:

  ACTIVE   → INACTIVE | ARCHIVED
  INACTIVE → ACTIVE | ARCHIVED
  ARCHIVED → terminal in the MVP

  INACTIVE preserves existing data. (Its former clause "prevents new
  gatherings" is withdrawn with gatherings, ADR-014; no replacement
  effect of INACTIVE is defined here.)

  ARCHIVED is rejected while active DISCIPLE memberships remain (the
  former "or DRAFT gatherings" clause is withdrawn, ADR-014).
  Disciples are never transferred automatically.

  See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 2.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 5a. D GROUP PLACEMENTS
// ============================================================

Table d_group_placements {
  id uuid [pk]

  d_group_id uuid [not null]
  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  started_at timestamptz [not null]
  ended_at timestamptz

  placed_by uuid [not null]
  ended_by uuid

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    church_membership_id [unique, note: 'WHERE ended_at IS NULL: one active placement per person']
    d_group_id [note: 'WHERE ended_at IS NULL']
  }

  Note: '''
  A person's membership of a D Group, independent of responsibility
  (Migration 012, ADR-018 decision 2). Active while ended_at IS NULL.

  CHECKs:

  - ended_at IS NULL OR ended_at > started_at
  - (ended_at IS NULL) = (ended_by IS NULL)

  Integrity trigger:

  - same church as the group
  - no overlapping placement periods for the same person
  - a placement cannot end while the person still holds an active
    responsibility (d_group_memberships) in the group

  The partial unique index enforces one active D Group per person
  declaratively, so two concurrent adds of the same person cannot
  both commit.

  An active placement with no active d_group_memberships row in the
  same group is the derived state "Needs setup"; it is never stored.
  "Unplaced" means no active placement. See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md
  sections 2 and 11.

  Created by create_d_group(), assign_d_group_leader() and
  add_members_to_d_group(); ended by remove_from_d_group() and by
  assign_d_group_leader() when the replaced Leader holds nothing else
  in the group. No client write grants. See RBAC_RLS_MATRIX.md
  section 3.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 6. D GROUP MEMBERSHIP / RESPONSIBILITY
// ============================================================

Table d_group_memberships {
  id uuid [pk]

  d_group_id uuid [not null]
  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  responsibility d_group_responsibility [not null]

  discipler_basis discipler_basis [note: 'required on DISCIPLER rows, NULL on LEADER and DISCIPLE rows (CHECK); Migration 013']

  started_at timestamptz [not null]
  ended_at timestamptz

  assigned_by uuid [not null]

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents historical responsibility inside a D Group. Membership
  of the group itself is d_group_placements (Migration 012): an
  active row here requires an active placement of the same person in
  the same D Group (integrity trigger).

  Responsibilities:

  LEADER
  DISCIPLER
  DISCIPLE

  ended_at = NULL means the assignment is active.

  Historical records are normally ended rather than overwritten
  or deleted.

  Example:

  James
  Young Adults A
  DISCIPLE
  January → Present

  James
  Young Adults A
  DISCIPLER
  September → Present (appointed after Lesson 5; his own journey
  continues, ADR-012)

  PostgreSQL must later enforce important active-state invariants such as:

  - one active Leader per D Group
  - one active D Group led per person
  - one active Disciple D Group per person
  - DISCIPLE and LEADER cannot be active simultaneously
  - ended_at IS NULL OR ended_at > started_at
  - no ambiguous overlapping periods for the same person,
    D Group and responsibility

  LEADER and DISCIPLER MAY be active simultaneously for the same
  person. A D Group Leader who personally disciples members must hold
  the DISCIPLER responsibility in order to receive discipler
  assignments.

  DISCIPLE and DISCIPLER MAY be active simultaneously for the same
  person, in the same D Group (ADR-012). DISCIPLE excludes LEADER.
  Enforced since Migration 013, which replaced the Migration 006
  integrity trigger body.

  discipler_basis records why a DISCIPLER row exists (ADR-018
  decision 6): INITIAL_ROLLOUT (set_up_member() while the church's
  initial setup window is open), LEADER_SELF (add_self_as_discipler())
  or APPOINTMENT (appoint_discipler(), with a ministry_role_transitions
  row). DISCIPLER rows that existed before Migration 013 were
  backfilled: LEADER_SELF where the holder led the group when the row
  started, INITIAL_ROLLOUT otherwise.

  Enforced since Migration 006, for every writer:

  - one active row per (church_membership_id, d_group_id,
    responsibility): partial unique index WHERE ended_at IS NULL
  - integrity trigger: same church as the group; no overlapping
    periods for the same person, group and responsibility; a
    person's responsibilities at any moment are all in one D Group;
    DISCIPLE cannot overlap LEADER (since Migration 013; Migration
    006 also refused DISCIPLE with DISCIPLER)
  - an active row requires an active placement in the same D Group
    (Migration 012)

  Rows are created only by create_d_group(),
  assign_d_group_leader(), set_up_member(), add_self_as_discipler()
  and appoint_discipler(), and ended by assign_d_group_leader()
  (LEADER) and remove_from_d_group() (every responsibility of the
  removed person). respond_to_d_group_invitation() and
  end_d_group_membership() were dropped by Migration 012.

  Overlap prevention matters because meeting participant and
  Discipler validity are resolved as of discipleship_meetings
  .occurred_at (DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 4). Ambiguous
  overlapping periods would make "did this person hold this
  responsibility at time T" undecidable. (The earlier rationale based
  on gathering.starts_at is withdrawn with gatherings, ADR-014.)
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 7. DISCIPLER → DISCIPLE ASSIGNMENT
// ============================================================

Table discipler_assignments {
  id uuid [pk]

  d_group_id uuid [not null]

  discipler_d_group_membership_id uuid [not null]
  disciple_d_group_membership_id uuid [not null]

  assigned_by uuid [not null]

  started_at timestamptz [not null]
  ended_at timestamptz

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents direct discipleship-care responsibility.

  Both referenced D Group membership records must belong to the
  same D Group.

  discipler_d_group_membership_id must represent DISCIPLER.

  disciple_d_group_membership_id must represent DISCIPLE.

  One Disciple may have at most one active primary Discipler.

  One Discipler may care for multiple Disciples.

  Self-pairing is prohibited: the two sides must belong to different
  church memberships (ADR-012 decision 6; integrity trigger since
  Migration 013). The CHECK below compares row ids only.

  Reciprocal pairing (A disciples B while B disciples A) is refused
  for overlapping periods (D7, ADR-018; integrity trigger since
  Migration 013).

  Reassignment ends the previous relationship and creates a new one.

  Historical assignments are preserved.

  Enforced since Migration 006: CHECK that the two sides differ, and
  an integrity trigger requiring the DISCIPLER side, the DISCIPLE
  side and both rows in this assignment's D Group, with no
  overlapping periods for the same Disciple. Written only by
  set_discipler() (pair, re-pair, unpair) and ended by
  remove_from_d_group() when either side is removed (Migration 012;
  end_d_group_membership() before it).
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 7a. D GROUP INVITATIONS
// ============================================================

Table d_group_invitations {
  id uuid [pk]

  d_group_id uuid [not null]
  church_membership_id uuid [not null, note: 'the invitee']

  responsibility d_group_responsibility [not null, note: 'DISCIPLER or DISCIPLE only']

  status d_group_invitation_status [not null, default: 'PENDING']

  invited_by uuid [not null]

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  expires_at timestamptz [not null, note: 'created_at + 14 days']
  responded_at timestamptz

  resulting_d_group_membership_id uuid

  indexes {
    church_membership_id [unique, note: 'WHERE status = PENDING']
    (d_group_id, status)
  }

  Note: '''
  Historical. Placement by invitation was retired by Migration 012
  (ADR-018 decision 1): pending rows were withdrawn (or marked EXPIRED
  when already overdue), invite_to_d_group(),
  withdraw_d_group_invitation(), respond_to_d_group_invitation(),
  get_my_pending_invitation() and
  private.expire_overdue_d_group_invitations() were dropped, and no
  operation writes this table any more. Placement is
  d_group_placements. The rest of this note describes the rows as
  Migration 006 wrote them.

  Confirmed placement. A Coordinator (any group) or the group's
  Leader invites an unplaced ACTIVE member as DISCIPLER or DISCIPLE;
  the member accepts or declines once. Accepting creates the
  d_group_memberships row and records it here. LEADER is never
  invited: it is a direct Coordinator appointment.

  A member holds at most one PENDING invitation.

  State CHECK:

  PENDING   → responded_at NULL, resulting NULL
  ACCEPTED  → responded_at and resulting set
  DECLINED / WITHDRAWN / EXPIRED → responded_at set, resulting NULL

  Expiry is lazy, with no scheduler. A PENDING row past expires_at
  is EXPIRED in meaning everywhere and is marked EXPIRED, with
  responded_at = expires_at, by the next successful write that
  touches it.

  Same church as the invitee, enforced by an integrity trigger.

  Written, until Migration 012, only by invite_to_d_group(),
  withdraw_d_group_invitation() and respond_to_d_group_invitation().
  See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md
  section 2 and RBAC_RLS_MATRIX.md section 3.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 8. D GROUP GATHERINGS (DEPRECATED, ADR-014)
// ============================================================

Table d_group_gatherings {
  id uuid [pk]

  d_group_id uuid [not null]

  title varchar
  topic varchar
  notes text

  starts_at timestamptz [not null]

  status gathering_status [not null, default: 'DRAFT']

  created_by uuid [not null]

  finalized_by uuid
  finalized_at timestamptz

  cancelled_by uuid
  cancelled_at timestamptz

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Deprecated: no MVP owner (ADR-014); retained in applied migrations;
  to be locked down by forward migration (revoke client grants); drop
  only after a separate decision.

  Current state: created by Migration 001 (set_updated_at trigger in
  Migration 002). RLS enabled with no policy; Supabase default grants
  (ALL to anon and authenticated) are still in place, so RLS
  deny-by-default is the only protection. Lock-down (revoke all from
  anon and authenticated, keep RLS, add no policy) is expected in the
  Slice 5 migration's grant step. Drop order, after a separate
  decision and an emptiness check: gathering_attendance, then this
  table, then gathering_status (ADR-014 decision 14). No RPC, policy,
  seed row or client reads or writes this table. Do not implement
  against it.

  Historical (ADR-014): the text below describes the withdrawn design
  and is kept for the record only. Gatherings have no MVP behaviour
  and feed no monitoring.

  Represented an actual overall D Group gathering, separate from a
  Discipleship Meeting: the D Group meeting together as a group.

  Historical lifecycle:

  DRAFT
  → attendance may be prepared/edited
  → does not affect official monitoring

  FINALIZED
  → gathering occurred
  → attendance becomes official
  → participates in attendance statistics and monitoring

  CANCELLED
  → gathering did not occur
  → excluded from attendance calculations and monitoring

  Allowed transitions:

  DRAFT → FINALIZED
  DRAFT → CANCELLED

  FINALIZED and CANCELLED are terminal in the MVP.

  FINALIZED → CANCELLED is NOT permitted. Retracting an already
  finalized gathering would silently withdraw official attendance
  from monitoring. If that capability is ever required it must be
  designed as its own controlled operation with explicit attendance
  and monitoring recalculation.

  CANCELLED → DRAFT and CANCELLED → FINALIZED are NOT permitted.

  State consistency:

  DRAFT
  → finalized_by / finalized_at NULL
  → cancelled_by / cancelled_at NULL

  FINALIZED
  → finalized_by / finalized_at NOT NULL
  → cancelled_by / cancelled_at NULL

  CANCELLED
  → cancelled_by / cancelled_at NOT NULL
  → finalized_by / finalized_at NULL

  Attendance recorded while the gathering was DRAFT is preserved
  when the gathering is cancelled. It is excluded from attendance
  metrics, absence monitoring and follow-up generation because the
  parent gathering status determines whether attendance is official.

  Finalization is an important business boundary.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 9. GATHERING ATTENDANCE (DEPRECATED, ADR-014)
// ============================================================

Table gathering_attendance {
  id uuid [pk]

  gathering_id uuid [not null]
  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  status attendance_status [not null]

  recorded_by uuid [not null]
  recorded_at timestamptz [not null]

  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    (gathering_id, church_membership_id) [unique]
  }

  Note: '''
  Deprecated: no MVP owner (ADR-014); retained in applied migrations;
  to be locked down by forward migration (revoke client grants); drop
  only after a separate decision.

  Current state: created by Migration 001 (set_updated_at trigger in
  Migration 002). RLS enabled with no policy; Supabase default grants
  (ALL to anon and authenticated) still in place. Lock-down and drop
  lifecycle as on d_group_gatherings (ADR-014 decision 14); this
  table is dropped first. No RPC, policy, seed row or client reads or
  writes it. Do not implement against it.

  Attendance in the MVP exists only as a discipleship meeting outcome
  (discipleship_meeting_participants.attendance_status, recorded
  through Record Meeting). There is no D Group, church-wide or event
  attendance and no attendance percentage. Monitoring never reads
  this table.

  Historical (ADR-014), kept for the record only: this table was the
  official attendance record for one church member at one D Group
  gathering, with PRESENT, LATE, ABSENT and EXCUSED outcomes,
  eligibility as of gathering.starts_at, a ban on recording one's own
  attendance, and controlled correction after finalization. It fed
  the withdrawn gathering-based CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE for LEADER and
  DISCIPLER subjects.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 10. CURRICULUM
// ============================================================

Table curricula {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_id uuid [not null]

  name varchar [not null]
  description text

  status curriculum_status [not null, default: 'ACTIVE']

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents a church-owned discipleship curriculum.

  The MVP uses one active fixed curriculum of ten lessons (user
  decision 2026-10-05). The count is seed data, not a rule: no rule
  or client hard-codes a lesson total.

  A church has at most one ACTIVE curriculum, enforced by a partial
  unique index. Discipler eligibility (ADR-012) and sequential progression both
  depend on "the active curriculum" being singular.

  Curriculum data is stored in PostgreSQL rather than being permanently
  hard-coded into Flutter.

  Lesson content (ADR-010, ADR-019): DiscipleTrack delivers the
  published lesson material in two tiers (Disciple; Discipler: answers,
  notes, modules), each gated by progression and relationship. It is
  published into Supabase by trusted tooling and cached on the device
  for offline reading, limited to what the person may read. Until
  permission to reproduce the source is confirmed, only identifying
  metadata is published and no lesson text is kept in the repository. Content is kept apart from progress, meetings and
  completion. Its table is not defined here yet: the Curriculum /
  Lesson Content slice adds it after inspecting the real material.
  Expected shape, not normative: a separate table keyed to
  curriculum_lessons.id with a published version, never columns that
  progress depends on.

  The earlier statement that DiscipleTrack "does not store or deliver
  lesson content" is superseded by ADR-010.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 11. CURRICULUM LESSONS
// ============================================================

Table curriculum_lessons {
  id uuid [pk]

  curriculum_id uuid [not null]

  lesson_number integer [not null]

  title varchar [not null]
  description text

  required_meetings integer [not null, default: 4]

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    (curriculum_id, lesson_number) [unique]
  }

  Note: '''
  Represents one ordered lesson in the curriculum.

  MVP:

  10 lessons, numbered 1 to 10 (user decision 2026-10-05).
  Migration 004 seeded twelve; the Slice 5 migration replaces
  private.bootstrap_church() and
  private.assert_bootstrap_postconditions() with the count changed
  to ten, and removes unreferenced Lessons 11 and 12 from existing
  ACTIVE curricula (DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 0).

  required_meetings (applied in Migration 001, seeded as 4) no
  longer drives progression (ADR-011). A lesson is completed
  when the Discipler marks it completed (ADR-015), not when a
  count is reached. There is no minimum number of meetings
  (ADR-017, N1 closed): this column is legacy data, read by no rule
  and denied to clients. It is not renamed and not dropped while the
  bootstrap functions still seed and assert it.

  Lesson progression is sequential.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 12. DISCIPLESHIP MEETINGS
// ============================================================

Table discipleship_meetings {
  id uuid [pk]

  d_group_id uuid [not null]

  discipler_d_group_membership_id uuid [not null]

  lesson_id uuid [not null]

  occurred_at timestamptz [not null]

  status discipleship_meeting_status [not null, default: 'RECORDED']

  notes text

  recorded_by uuid [not null]

  voided_by uuid
  voided_at timestamptz

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents one discipleship meeting reported by the Discipler after
  the fact, with each expected Disciple's recorded outcome.

  Real-world meaning: the Discipler and assigned Disciple(s) arrange
  their meetups themselves, outside the app. DiscipleTrack records what
  actually happened. It does not schedule meetings, and no record exists
  before the meetup.

  The meeting may be:

  - one-on-one
  - small group

  Each meeting has:

  - one responsible Discipler
  - one lesson
  - one or more expected Disciples, each with an attendance outcome
    recorded in discipleship_meeting_participants

  The derived "held meetup" and "missed meetup" labels are withdrawn
  (ADR-014 decision 8). A meeting record shows each Disciple's
  recorded outcome. A RECORDED meeting in which every outcome is
  EXCUSED is not an absence: it has no credited participation and
  breaks the consecutive recorded absence streak. When no meeting is
  recorded, nothing is inferred: NO RECORD is not ABSENT.

  RECORDED:
  → meeting is valid
  → credited participants contribute toward progress

  VOIDED:
  → meeting remains historically preserved
  → contributes nothing toward lesson progress or monitoring

  occurred_at is when the meeting took place, or, where every
  recorded outcome is ABSENT or EXCUSED, when it was arranged to take
  place. It must not be in the future.

  Historical validity should use occurred_at rather than the date
  on which the record was entered.

  This allows legitimate backdated meeting entry.

  The Discipler is the normal recorder. A Leader or Coordinator may
  record on behalf of the responsible Discipler as a fallback;
  recorded_by distinguishes who entered the record.

  d_group_id, discipler_d_group_membership_id, lesson_id, occurred_at
  and recorded_by are immutable. The only status transition is
  RECORDED → VOIDED, which is terminal. Corrections use void and
  re-record. See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 4.

  notes are shared meeting notes, visible to the meeting's
  participants. Pastoral care observations belong in
  follow_up_actions.

  occurred_at is immutable after creation. Every participant validity
  check is evaluated as of occurred_at, so editing it would silently
  invalidate checks already performed. Corrections use void and
  re-record rather than changing the timestamp.

  The Discipler must have held the appropriate responsibility when
  the meeting actually occurred.

  A meeting records exactly one lesson, so every RECORDED participant,
  whatever their attendance outcome, must be eligible for that same
  lesson under sequential progression.

  Disciples who are on different lessons therefore require separate
  discipleship meeting records. This is an intentional ministry
  constraint, not a modelling limitation.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 13. DISCIPLESHIP MEETING PARTICIPANTS
// ============================================================

Table discipleship_meeting_participants {
  id uuid [pk]

  meeting_id uuid [not null]

  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  status meeting_participation_status [not null, default: 'RECORDED']

  attendance_status attendance_status [not null, note: 'Explicit outcome; no default']

  voided_by uuid
  voided_at timestamptz

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    (meeting_id, church_membership_id) [unique]
  }

  Note: '''
  Records each Disciple who was expected at a meetup, and what
  happened for that Disciple.

  "Expected" means listed by the recorder when the meetup is recorded.
  There is no prior schedule.

  Real-world meaning: discipleship meeting attendance records each
  Disciple's explicitly recorded outcome in their lesson-based
  discipleship meetings. It is the only attendance in the MVP
  (ADR-014). No consistency ratio or percentage is derived from it
  (decision 8).

  status is record validity only:

  RECORDED:
  → valid participant record

  VOIDED:
  → remains historically preserved
  → contributes nothing toward progress or monitoring

  attendance_status is the outcome, supplied explicitly by the recorder:

  PRESENT → credited
  LATE    → credited
  ABSENT  → not credited
  EXCUSED → not credited

  A participation is CREDITED, and contributes one meeting toward the
  Disciple's lesson, only when:

  - the meeting status is RECORDED
  - the participant status is RECORDED
  - attendance_status is PRESENT or LATE

  An absence never increments progress. It remains visible in the
  Disciple's meeting history and drives the consecutive recorded
  absence condition (CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE, ADR-014).

  The meeting ordinal shown to users, such as "Meeting 3", is derived by
  ordering credited participations for the lesson by
  (occurred_at, meeting id). Uncredited rows are not numbered.

  Recording a meeting never changes lesson readiness or completion by
  itself (ADR-011). The credited count is factual history.

  church_membership_id, attendance_status and the parent context are
  immutable. The only transition is RECORDED → VOIDED. An incorrect
  outcome is corrected by voiding and re-recording the meeting. Because
  (meeting_id, church_membership_id) is unique including VOIDED rows, a
  voided participant is never re-added to the same meeting.

  Participant identity references church_membership_id rather than
  temporary D Group membership.

  This allows progress to survive:

  - D Group transfer
  - Discipler reassignment

  A RECORDED participant, whatever the outcome, is only valid when, as
  of the meeting's occurred_at, the participant held an active DISCIPLE
  responsibility in the meeting's D Group and an active discipler
  assignment to the meeting's Discipler.

  A Disciple with no assigned Discipler therefore cannot receive
  progress credit, or have an absence recorded, until an assignment
  exists.

  See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 4 for the exact rules.

  Example:

  Mark meets James and Anna for Lesson 4. James attends; Anna does not.

  Before:

  James = 2 meetings recorded for Lesson 4
  Anna  = 1 meeting recorded for Lesson 4

  Recorded:

  James PRESENT → credited
  Anna  ABSENT  → not credited

  After:

  James = 3 meetings recorded
  Anna  = 1 meeting recorded, and one recorded absence in her history

  Their progress remains independent.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 14. DISCIPLE LESSON PROGRESS
// ============================================================

Table disciple_lesson_progress {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  lesson_id uuid [not null]

  status lesson_progress_status [not null, default: 'NOT_STARTED', note: 'READY_FOR_COMPLETION is not entered by any operation (ADR-015)']

  started_at timestamptz
  ready_at timestamptz [note: 'ADR-015: equals completed_at for a completion; READY_FOR_COMPLETION is no longer entered']
  submitted_by uuid [note: 'profiles.id; added by the Slice 5 migration (ADR-011). Under ADR-015 set by complete_lesson() to the same person as confirmed_by; cleared by undo and reopen']

  completed_at timestamptz
  confirmed_by uuid [note: 'profiles.id of who marked the lesson completed (ADR-015); no Leader confirmation']

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    (church_membership_id, lesson_id) [unique]
  }

  Note: '''
  Represents the individual Disciple's lesson lifecycle.

  IMPORTANT:

  Progress belongs to the person's church journey.

  It does NOT belong to their temporary D Group assignment.

  Therefore progress survives:

  - D Group transfer
  - Discipler reassignment
  - Leader changes

  Expected lifecycle (ADR-011, revised by ADR-015):

  NOT_STARTED
      ↓ first credited participation (derived)

  IN_PROGRESS
      ↓ the Discipler marks the lesson completed (explicit;
        complete_lesson(); the Leader of the current D Group or the
        Coordinator as fallback)

  COMPLETED

  There is no Leader confirmation (ADR-015). READY_FOR_COMPLETION
  stays in the lesson_progress_status enum (Migration 001 is
  immutable) but no operation enters it.

  No meeting count completes a lesson. The meeting count is derived
  from credited participation and is factual history. ABSENT and
  EXCUSED outcomes never advance progress. There is no upper limit on
  meetings per lesson.

  A missing progress row may also be interpreted as NOT_STARTED
  during implementation, avoiding unnecessary empty rows.

  LOCKED is a derived UI/domain state and is not stored here.

  Timestamp semantics:

  started_at
  → occurred_at of the earliest credited participation for
    this lesson

  started_at is recomputed when backdated participation changes the
  chronology.

  completed_at, confirmed_by
  → when, and by whom, the lesson was marked completed.

  ready_at, submitted_by
  → reused without a schema change (ADR-015): complete_lesson() sets
    ready_at = completed_at and submitted_by = the same person as
    confirmed_by, so the Progress State Check (all five fields set for
    COMPLETED) is unchanged.

  All four are cleared by undo_lesson_completion() and by
  reopen_lesson_completion().

  Marking completed needs only the lesson to be the eligible
  (current) lesson, NOT_STARTED or IN_PROGRESS; meeting count is never
  a condition (ADR-017). With no credited participation, started_at is
  set to completed_at. See DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 4.

  Undo (ADR-015): the current assigned Discipler, the Leader of the
  current D Group or the Coordinator, only while the lesson is the
  person's latest COMPLETED lesson and no meeting has been recorded
  for them on the next lesson. Returns to IN_PROGRESS (NOT_STARTED
  when no credited participation remains); audited with the prior
  values.

  COMPLETED is protected. A void never changes a COMPLETED status and
  is never refused to protect one (ADR-017): recomputation leaves
  COMPLETED as it is. A completed lesson changes only through undo
  while its window holds, or the database-only reopen (ADR-016). Voiding an uncredited (ABSENT or EXCUSED)
  row never affects progress, and a void is never used to unlock a
  completion.

  Reopening (Coordinator only) is a database-level recovery operation
  with no MVP action (ADR-016). It never cascades. It is permitted only
  when no later lesson is COMPLETED for the same church membership, no
  later lesson has a recorded meeting for the person, and, for a person
  appointed as a Discipler, the lesson is later than
  the eligibility lesson (Lesson 5): the eligibility lesson and
  earlier lessons cannot be reopened for an appointed person; later
  lessons can, subject to the other preconditions (ADR-012 decision 9,
  N9; the eligibility lesson comes from
  private.discipler_eligibility_lesson(), D2). It returns the lesson
  to IN_PROGRESS, or NOT_STARTED when no credited participation
  remains, never to READY_FOR_COMPLETION (ADR-015); the prior
  completion stays recoverable through audit_events.

  Discipler eligibility is derived from COMPLETED of Lesson 5 of the
  active curriculum, marked by the Discipler (ADR-015; completed_at is
  the "eligible since" date). It is never stored here (ADR-012).

  READY_FOR_COMPLETION is dormant (ADR-015): nothing enters it, and
  with no minimum (ADR-017) a row found in it keeps it.

  Progression is sequential: lesson N requires lesson N-1 COMPLETED.
  Lesson 1 is exempt.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 15. MINISTRY ROLE TRANSITIONS
// ============================================================

Table ministry_role_transitions {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_membership_id uuid [not null]
  d_group_id uuid [not null]

  from_responsibility d_group_responsibility [not null]
  to_responsibility d_group_responsibility [not null]

  approved_by uuid [not null]
  approved_at timestamptz [not null]

  notes text

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Historical record of significant D Group responsibility changes.
  The table name is kept; in the MVP a row is the Discipler
  appointment record (ADR-012).

  MVP primary use: Discipler appointment

  from_responsibility = DISCIPLE
  to_responsibility   = DISCIPLER

  The row records that a Disciple was appointed as a Discipler. It
  does NOT mean the DISCIPLE responsibility ended: the person keeps
  their DISCIPLE row, their own discipler assignment and their lesson
  progress, and continues through Lesson 10 (ADR-012 decision 4).

  Appointment requires:

  - eligibility: Lesson 5 of the church's ACTIVE curriculum at
    COMPLETED, marked by the Discipler (ADR-015; IN_PROGRESS does not
    count); derived, never stored (ADR-012 decision 1; the lesson comes
    from private.discipler_eligibility_lesson(), D2)
  - a direct Coordinator appointment, with no acceptance workflow;
    attributed (approved_by, approved_at) and audited

  Eligibility does not automatically cause appointment.

  d_group_id preserves the organizational context. The DISCIPLER
  responsibility is created in the same D Group as the person's
  DISCIPLE responsibility (ADR-012 decision 3).

  The appointment happens transactionally:

  validate eligibility
      ↓
  create DISCIPLER responsibility (DISCIPLE row not ended)
      ↓
  record appointment

  All succeed or all fail. Written only by appoint_discipler()
  (Migration 014), which creates the DISCIPLER row with
  discipler_basis APPOINTMENT; no client write grants. The table is
  the appointment record only: initial rollout recognition
  (discipler_basis INITIAL_ROLLOUT) and a Leader adding themselves
  (LEADER_SELF) write no row here (ADR-018 decision 6), so neither
  counts as an appointment for the reopen and undo protections
  (DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 4). The earlier "promotion" design
  (eligibility after every lesson, ending the DISCIPLE
  responsibility) is superseded by ADR-012.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 16. ATTENTION CONDITIONS
// ============================================================

Table attention_conditions {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  d_group_id uuid

  condition_type attention_condition_type [not null]

  status attention_condition_status [not null, default: 'ACTIVE']

  detected_at timestamptz [not null]
  resolved_at timestamptz

  metadata jsonb

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents a system-detected condition requiring possible
  human attention.

  The single MVP condition, one source, one threshold (ADR-014):

  CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE
  → source: explicitly recorded ABSENT outcomes on RECORDED
    participant rows of RECORDED discipleship meetings
  → subjects: DISCIPLE responsibilities only
  → episode: the current discipler assignment
  → order: (occurred_at, meeting id), across lesson boundaries
  → ABSENT increments; PRESENT and LATE break; EXCUSED breaks and is
    never an absence (BR-027a)
  → threshold: church_settings.consecutive_absence_threshold
  → user-facing label: "consecutive recorded absences"

  Monitoring uses only explicitly recorded facts. NO RECORD is not
  ABSENT. No condition is ever generated from elapsed time,
  inactivity or the lack of a record. The withdrawn
  CONSECUTIVE_MISSED_MEETINGS, the gathering-based condition for
  LEADER and DISCIPLER subjects and any inactivity condition are not
  part of the design (ADR-014). Leaders and Disciplers have no
  automated monitoring in the MVP; a person who is both a Disciple
  and a Discipler is monitored as a Disciple only.

  At most one ACTIVE condition per (church_membership_id,
  condition_type), by partial unique index.

  Recalculation runs after record_discipleship_meeting(),
  void_discipleship_meeting(), void_meeting_participant(),
  set_discipler() / remove_from_d_group() and transfer_disciple().
  Discipler appointment is not a trigger (ADR-012).

  This table answers:

  "What condition did the system detect?"

  It does NOT represent the human care workflow itself.

  Example:

  Juan, a Disciple, has 3 consecutive recorded ABSENT outcomes in
  meetings recorded by his Discipler.

  Monitoring creates:

  CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE
  ACTIVE

  When a later outcome breaks the streak:

  ACTIVE
      ↓
  RESOLVED

  If another absence episode occurs later, a NEW condition is
  created rather than reopening the historical condition.

  Each condition is one episode and produces at most one follow-up.
  A new episode may therefore generate a new follow-up even while an
  earlier follow-up is still unresolved.

  Ending an episode resolves any ACTIVE condition belonging to it, in
  the same transaction. That happens when the discipler assignment
  ends, including through Discipler reassignment and D Group
  transfer. Discipler appointment does not end the assignment and is
  not an episode boundary (ADR-012). The new episode starts with a
  fresh streak.

  metadata may contain supporting detection context, but must not
  replace normalized source records.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 17. FOLLOW-UPS
// ============================================================

Table follow_ups {
  id uuid [pk]

  attention_condition_id uuid [not null]

  church_membership_id uuid [not null]
  d_group_id uuid

  assignee_church_membership_id uuid [not null]

  reason_type follow_up_reason [not null]

  status follow_up_status [not null, default: 'REQUIRED']

  due_at timestamptz

  resolved_at timestamptz
  resolved_by uuid

  resolution_type follow_up_resolution_type
  resolution_note text

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Represents a human ministry-care case.

  This table answers:

  "Who is responsible for responding to this concern?"

  assignee_church_membership_id is a responsibility field, not an
  actor field. It records the person currently accountable for the
  care, so it uses church_memberships.id rather than profiles.id.

  Episode identity:

  An attention_condition represents one absence episode. A follow-up
  is the care case for exactly one episode.

  attention_condition_id is NOT NULL and UNIQUE, so one detected
  condition produces at most one follow-up. Retrying creation for the
  same condition cannot create a second case, which is what makes
  monitoring idempotent.

  A new episode creates a new condition and therefore may create a new
  follow-up even while an earlier follow-up remains unresolved. A
  person may consequently have several simultaneously open follow-ups,
  one per episode. This is intended.

  reason_type is retained for read and query convenience. Monitoring
  must keep it consistent with the originating condition's
  condition_type.

  Assignment escalation, evaluated on distinct people:

  Subject is DISCIPLE
  → active primary Discipler
  → otherwise D Group Leader
  → otherwise Coordinator

  Subject is DISCIPLER
  → D Group Leader
  → otherwise Coordinator

  Subject is LEADER
  → Coordinator

  The DISCIPLE branch is triggered by CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE, the single
  MVP condition (ADR-014). The DISCIPLER and LEADER branches have no
  MVP trigger (ADR-014); they are kept as architecture and are not
  built until a factual condition for those subjects is approved.
  Every gathering-derived input is removed.

  A follow-up is never assigned to its own subject. Where one person
  holds several responsibilities, the chain continues until a
  different person is reached.

  The escalation chain terminates at Coordinator, so the church must
  preserve at least one active COORDINATOR.

  Lifecycle:

  REQUIRED
      ↓ first real follow-up action
  IN_PROGRESS
      ↓ resolution
  RESOLVED

  OVERDUE is derived rather than stored:

  status != RESOLVED
  AND due_at < now()

  Resolving a follow-up does NOT automatically mean the underlying
  attention condition disappeared.

  The condition and human care workflow have independent lifecycles.

  The reverse also holds: when monitoring resolves the underlying
  attention condition because a later recorded outcome broke the
  streak, the follow-up is
  NOT auto-closed. The care obligation remains until someone resolves
  it deliberately, normally with resolution_type CONDITION_CORRECTED.

  Queries and UI should distinguish:

  - open follow-up with an ACTIVE condition
  - open follow-up whose condition is already RESOLVED

  resolution_type provides structured resolution semantics.

  Existing assignments should not silently change when the Disciple's
  Discipler changes. Reassignment of an open follow-up should be
  deliberate and auditable.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 18. FOLLOW-UP ACTIONS
// ============================================================

Table follow_up_actions {
  id uuid [pk]

  follow_up_id uuid [not null]

  action_type follow_up_action_type [not null]

  note text

  performed_by uuid [not null]
  performed_at timestamptz [not null]

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Stores historical care actions performed during a follow-up.

  Examples:

  SENT_MESSAGE
  CALLED
  PERSONAL_CONVERSATION
  SCHEDULED_VISIT

  Example history:

  Sep 19
  SENT_MESSAGE
  "Asked James how he is doing."

  Sep 20
  CALLED
  "Work schedule has been difficult."

  Sep 22
  PERSONAL_CONVERSATION
  "Agreed to meet his Discipler next week."

  Actions remain historically preserved after the follow-up is
  resolved.

  Sensitive care notes require strict RLS authorization.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 19. ANNOUNCEMENTS
// ============================================================

Table announcements {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_id uuid [not null]

  d_group_id uuid

  author_id uuid [not null]

  scope announcement_scope [not null]

  title varchar [not null]
  body text [not null]

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Lightweight church and D Group communication.

  CHURCH:

  d_group_id must be NULL.

  Normal publishing authority:
  Coordinator.

  D_GROUP:

  d_group_id must be present.

  Normal publishing authority:
  relevant D Group Leader.

  MVP intentionally does NOT include:

  - comments
  - reactions
  - likes
  - chat
  - read receipts

  Announcements are communication, not a social network.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 20. CHURCH SETTINGS
// ============================================================

Table church_settings {
  church_id uuid [pk]

  consecutive_absence_threshold integer [not null, default: 3]

  consecutive_missed_meeting_threshold integer [not null, default: 3]

  follow_up_due_days integer

  initial_setup_closed_at timestamptz [note: 'NULL while the initial setup window is open; Migration 013']

  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Stores church-level configurable business behavior.

  initial_setup_closed_at (ADR-018 decision 5): while NULL, the
  church's initial setup window is open and set_up_member() may
  recognize an Existing Discipler (discipler_basis INITIAL_ROLLOUT).
  Set and cleared only by set_initial_setup_open() (Coordinator,
  audited). Once set, DISCIPLER comes only from appointment, apart
  from a Leader adding themselves.

  Initial MVP defaults:

  consecutive_absence_threshold = 3
  → the number of consecutive explicitly recorded ABSENT discipleship
    meeting outcomes, within the current discipler assignment, that
    raises CONSECUTIVE_ABSENCE for a Disciple. Redefined by ADR-014
    decision 12; it previously applied to gathering attendance. The
    single monitoring threshold. Migration 004 asserts the bootstrap
    default 3 (whether 3 stays the default is open, ADR-014).

  consecutive_missed_meeting_threshold = 3
  → dormant, no owner (ADR-014 decision 13); bootstrap still inserts
    and asserts it. Added by Migration 004 for the withdrawn
    CONSECUTIVE_MISSED_MEETINGS condition, whose name its database
    comment still carries. No rule reads it. Dropping it requires
    replacing private.bootstrap_church() and
    private.assert_bootstrap_postconditions() in the same forward
    migration and updating test/integration/bootstrap_test.dart; not
    dropped now.

  The earlier "two independent thresholds" design is withdrawn
  (ADR-014): one condition, one source, one threshold.

  This prevents important business thresholds from being permanently
  hard-coded into Flutter.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 21. AUDIT EVENTS
// ============================================================

Table audit_events {
  id uuid [pk]

  church_id uuid

  actor_user_id uuid [not null]

  action varchar [not null]

  entity_type varchar [not null]
  entity_id uuid

  metadata jsonb

  created_at timestamptz [not null]

  Note: '''
  Supplemental audit log for important operations.

  Useful examples:

  - meeting void
  - participant void
  - follow-up reassignment
  - administrative correction
  - privileged configuration change

  Do NOT duplicate every domain record into this table.

  Domain tables already preserve fields such as:

  created_by
  recorded_by
  assigned_by
  approved_by
  confirmed_by
  performed_by
  resolved_by
  voided_by

  Audit events supplement domain history rather than replacing it.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// 22. PRIVATE SCHEMA: JOIN CODE ATTEMPTS
// ============================================================
//
// The `private` schema holds database objects that must never have
// a PostgREST endpoint: bootstrap, RLS helper functions and the
// rate-limit state below. It is not listed in the API schemas.
// ============================================================

Table private.join_code_attempts {
  id uuid [pk]

  user_id uuid [not null]

  kind varchar [not null, note: 'LOOKUP or REQUEST']

  attempted_at timestamptz [not null]

  indexes {
    (user_id, attempted_at)
  }

  Note: '''
  Security infrastructure, not domain data.

  One row per join-code lookup or join request by an authenticated
  user. lookup_church_by_join_code() and request_join_church() refuse
  with PT429 when the caller has 5 attempts of any kind in the last
  10 minutes, or 3 REQUEST attempts in the last hour
  (DATABASE_CONSTRAINTS.md section 1).

  user_id is the auth user id with no foreign key, so the log never
  blocks account deletion. Rows older than 24 hours are pruned
  opportunistically on each call.

  RLS is enabled with no policies and no client role holds any grant.
  '''
}


// ============================================================
// RELATIONSHIPS
// ============================================================


// ============================================================
// Identity / Church
// ============================================================

Ref: church_memberships.user_id > profiles.id

Ref: church_memberships.church_id > churches.id

Ref: church_memberships.approved_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// System Roles
// ============================================================

Ref: church_role_assignments.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: church_role_assignments.assigned_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// D Groups
// ============================================================

Ref: d_groups.church_id > churches.id

Ref: d_groups.created_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// D Group Placements
// ============================================================

Ref: d_group_placements.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: d_group_placements.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: d_group_placements.placed_by > profiles.id

Ref: d_group_placements.ended_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// D Group Membership
// ============================================================

Ref: d_group_memberships.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: d_group_memberships.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: d_group_memberships.assigned_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Discipler Assignment
// ============================================================

Ref: discipler_assignments.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: discipler_assignments.discipler_d_group_membership_id > d_group_memberships.id

Ref: discipler_assignments.disciple_d_group_membership_id > d_group_memberships.id

Ref: discipler_assignments.assigned_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// D Group Invitations
// ============================================================

Ref: d_group_invitations.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: d_group_invitations.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: d_group_invitations.invited_by > profiles.id

Ref: d_group_invitations.resulting_d_group_membership_id > d_group_memberships.id


// ============================================================
// D Group Gatherings (deprecated, ADR-014; table still exists)
// ============================================================

Ref: d_group_gatherings.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: d_group_gatherings.created_by > profiles.id

Ref: d_group_gatherings.finalized_by > profiles.id

Ref: d_group_gatherings.cancelled_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Gathering Attendance (deprecated, ADR-014; table still exists)
// ============================================================

Ref: gathering_attendance.gathering_id > d_group_gatherings.id

Ref: gathering_attendance.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: gathering_attendance.recorded_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Curriculum
// ============================================================

Ref: curricula.church_id > churches.id

Ref: curriculum_lessons.curriculum_id > curricula.id


// ============================================================
// Discipleship Meetings
// ============================================================

Ref: discipleship_meetings.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: discipleship_meetings.discipler_d_group_membership_id > d_group_memberships.id

Ref: discipleship_meetings.lesson_id > curriculum_lessons.id

Ref: discipleship_meetings.recorded_by > profiles.id

Ref: discipleship_meetings.voided_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Discipleship Meeting Participants
// ============================================================

Ref: discipleship_meeting_participants.meeting_id > discipleship_meetings.id

Ref: discipleship_meeting_participants.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: discipleship_meeting_participants.voided_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Lesson Progress
// ============================================================

Ref: disciple_lesson_progress.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: disciple_lesson_progress.lesson_id > curriculum_lessons.id

Ref: disciple_lesson_progress.confirmed_by > profiles.id

Ref: disciple_lesson_progress.submitted_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Ministry Role Transitions
// ============================================================

Ref: ministry_role_transitions.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: ministry_role_transitions.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: ministry_role_transitions.approved_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Attention Conditions
// ============================================================

Ref: attention_conditions.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: attention_conditions.d_group_id > d_groups.id


// ============================================================
// Follow-ups
// ============================================================

Ref: follow_ups.attention_condition_id > attention_conditions.id

Ref: follow_ups.church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: follow_ups.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: follow_ups.assignee_church_membership_id > church_memberships.id

Ref: follow_ups.resolved_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Follow-up Actions
// ============================================================

Ref: follow_up_actions.follow_up_id > follow_ups.id

Ref: follow_up_actions.performed_by > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Announcements
// ============================================================

Ref: announcements.church_id > churches.id

Ref: announcements.d_group_id > d_groups.id

Ref: announcements.author_id > profiles.id


// ============================================================
// Church Settings
// ============================================================

Ref: church_settings.church_id - churches.id


// ============================================================
// Audit Events
// ============================================================

Ref: audit_events.church_id > churches.id

Ref: audit_events.actor_user_id > profiles.id